WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.000 --> 00:01:07.000
Almost daily, the news is full of stories of high school and college seniors missing out on their graduation ceremonies and of people being unable to see and touch their loved ones who are suffering from COVID-19. Some of the more heartbreaking accounts feature families having to bury loved ones without funerals or formal mourning. The coronavirus is keeping us from experiencing some of the deepest and most meaningful rituals of our lives. What is this doing to us psychologically? How important are rituals to our mental health and well-being? What new rituals might we be creating? Welcome to Speaking of Psychology, the flagship podcast of the American Psychological Association that explores the links between psychological science and everyday life. I'm Kim Mills. Our guest today is Dr. Michael I. Norton, a professor of business administration at the Harvard Business School, and a member of Harvard's Behavioral Insights Group. His research covers a gamut of fascinating topics from money to daydreaming, from greed to deception.
几乎每天，新闻里都充斥着高中和大学毕业生错过毕业典礼的故事，以及人们无法探望和触摸因新冠肺炎而痛苦的挚友亲朋。一些更令人心碎的故事包括家庭在没有葬礼或正式哀悼的情况下埋葬亲人。冠状病毒使我们无法体验生活中一些最深刻、最有意义的仪式。这在心理上对我们造成了什么影响？仪式对我们的心理健康和幸福有多重要？我们可能会创造什么新仪式？欢迎收看《心理学演讲》，这是美国心理学协会的旗舰播客，探讨心理科学与日常生活之间的联系。我是金·米尔斯。我们今天的嘉宾是哈佛商学院商业管理教授、哈佛行为洞察小组成员迈克尔·I·诺顿博士。他的研究涵盖了从金钱到白日梦、从贪婪到欺骗等一系列迷人的主题。

2
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:38.000
But I asked him here today to talk primarily about rituals in light of the COVID-19 pandemic. Welcome to speaking of psychology, Dr. Norton. Thank you so much for inviting me. I think we probably all have an idea of what rituals are, but it always helps to have a common language. Is it, for example, a ritual to take a coffee break every day at 1015, or is that just a habit? What are the differences between rituals and habits? Could you define for our listeners what constitutes a ritual? There certainly lie on a continuum.
但我今天请他来主要是为了讨论鉴于新冠肺炎疫情的仪式。欢迎来到心理学演讲，诺顿博士。非常感谢邀请我。我想我们大概都知道仪式是什么，但拥有共同的语言总是很有帮助的。例如，每天10点15分喝杯咖啡算不算仪式，还是仅仅是一个习惯？仪式和习惯有什么区别？你能为我们的听众定义一下什么是仪式吗？它们当然存在于一个连续体上。

3
00:01:38.000 --> 00:02:14.000
So there are a lot of habits that have some ritualistic elements. And there are a lot of rituals that, of course, look a lot like habits. So it's hard to differentiate them into pure, that's purely a habit and that's purely a ritual. But one way to think about that continuum is, if I ask you in the morning, do you first brush your teeth and then take a shower? or do you first take a shower and then brush your teeth? Crazily, when I asked that question, in countries all over the world, actually, about 50% of people do it one way and 50% of people do it the other way. So they have this thing they do every morning,
所以有许多习惯包含一些仪式性的元素。也有许多仪式，当然，看起来很像习惯。因此，很难将它们区分为纯粹是习惯和纯粹是仪式。但思考这个连续体的一种方法是，如果我早上问你，你是先刷牙然后洗澡？还是先洗澡然后刷牙？疯狂的是，当我问这个问题时，实际上在世界各地的国家中，大约50%的人以一种方式做，50%的人以另一种方式做。所以他们每天早上都有这样做的事，

4
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:42.000
and they really think their way is the good way and the other way is the bad way. Now then if I ask, imagine in your head, and you can do this yourself, imagine in your head doing them in the opposite order tomorrow morning, like really simulate that you would, instead of brushing your teeth first, you'd get in the shower first. When I ask people that, a lot of people make a face, like I don't like that. That feels weird to me or that feels wrong to me. But a lot of people say, no problem at all, I'm happy to switch the order.
而且他们真的认为自己的方式是好方式，而另一种方式是坏方式。现在，如果我问，在脑海中想象一下，你也可以自己做这个，想象明天早上以相反的顺序做这些事，就像真正模拟你一样，你会先进淋浴间，而不是先刷牙。当我问人们这个问题时，很多人会做个鬼脸，好像我不喜欢那样。这对我来说感觉很奇怪或感觉不对。但很多人说，没问题，我很高兴切换顺序。

5
00:02:42.000 --> 00:03:13.000
You can see in there sort of the difference between a habit and a ritual. So if you like to do things a certain way, but then if I say switch it, you don't mind. It's more like a habit. In the morning, you need to brush your teeth, need to take a shower. You have a habit of doing that. The order doesn't really matter. But if you're one of those people who felt a little weird when you thought about doing it differently, now that's a little bit further down the continuum toward a ritual because now the order in which you do things really matters to you. When it really shouldn't matter necessarily, it feels like it matters.
你可以在其中看到习惯和仪式之间的某种区别。所以如果你喜欢以某种方式做事，但如果我说切换它，你不介意。这更像是一个习惯。早上，你需要刷牙，需要洗澡。你有这样做的习惯。顺序其实并不重要。但如果你是那些在想到以不同方式做事时感到有点奇怪的人之一，那么这就更进一步接近仪式了，因为现在你做事所用的顺序对你来说真的很重要。当它其实不一定非要重要时，它感觉很重要。

