00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:48.000
And that means that it's a little bit more important to you. So rituals seem to be ubiquitous. I mean, I can't think of a culture or a time in history when humans did not engage in rituals. They can seem trivial, like buying a weekly lottery ticket, or they can be really profound, like a funeral mass. So my question is, are we physically hardwired to perform rituals? What does the science tell us about where this drive comes from? One of the interesting things about studying rituals is, of course, that many different disciplines have tackled the topic. I'm certainly not the first person to start studying rituals.
这意味着它对你来说稍微更重要一些。所以仪式似乎无处不在。我的意思是，我想不出人类不参与仪式的人类文化或历史时期。它们看起来可能微不足道，比如每周买一张彩票，或者它们可能非常深远，比如葬礼弥撒。所以我的问题是，我们在生理上是天生注定要进行仪式的吗？科学告诉我们这种驱动力来自哪里？研究仪式有趣的事情之一，当然是许多不同的学科都探讨了这个话题。我当然不是第一个开始研究仪式的人。

7
00:03:48.000 --> 00:04:23.000
So if you look in anthropology and sociology, of course, in clinical psychology, which I hope we'll talk about as well, people have been looking at what rituals are and how ubiquitous they are. And in fact, if you think about how we identify that some humans had some culture way back when, we often look to see if they buried their dead in a ceremonious fashion or not. So if they don't, then we say maybe they didn't have culture in a sense. But when there's shells around the body, we use that to say they must have felt differently about death. They must have had beliefs about death. They must have wanted to honor that person as a group.
所以如果你看看人类学和社会学，当然还有临床心理学，我希望我们也会讨论，人们一直在研究仪式是什么以及它们是多么无处不在。事实上，如果你想想我们是如何识别很久以前的一些人类有某种文化的，我们经常去看看他们是否以一种隆重的方式埋葬了他们的死者。所以如果他们没有，那么我们就会说也许他们在某种意义上没有文化。但当尸体周围有贝壳时，我们用它来证明他们对死亡的感受一定有所不同。他们一定对死亡有信仰。他们一定想作为一个群体来纪念那个人。

8
00:04:23.000 --> 00:05:08.000
So rituals, in a way, are one of the ways we identify when humans became human. So sheltering in place and needing to keep at least six feet away from most other people are actions that have radically altered our lives and prevented us from doing so many things. What's the long-term impact of missing out on some of the rituals I mentioned earlier, especially not being able to fully grieve loved ones we've lost to the coronavirus? I think our research would suggest that there is good news and bad news. So one of the first projects that we did on rituals I did with my colleague Francesca Gino on grieving rituals. And one of the things we saw is we sort of thought we would be studying grief in terms of the public communal rituals.
所以仪式在某种程度上是我们识别人类何时成为人类的方法之一。因此，居家隔离和需要与大多数其他人保持至少六英尺的距离是极大地改变了我们的生活并阻止我们做很多事情的行动。错过我之前提到的一些仪式的长期影响是什么，特别是无法充分哀悼我们因冠状病毒而失去的亲人？我认为我们的研究表明有好消息也有坏消息。所以我们对仪式进行的第一批研究项目之一是我与我的同事弗兰西斯卡·吉诺合作关于哀悼仪式的研究。我们看到的事情之一是我们原本以为我们会从公众公共仪式的角度来研究悲伤。

9
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:31.000
We all think of often religious rituals as well. But when we asked people, you know, think of someone who you love to have passed away. and we asked them just, what did you do after that? Many people wrote something like, there was a funeral, but then they would write something very private that they did just for that person just by themselves that often no one else even knew about. One of the examples that always stays with me
我们都经常想到宗教仪式。但当我们问人们时，你知道的，想想你爱的人已经去世了。我们只是问他们，在那之后你做了什么？许多人写了一些东西，比如，有一个葬礼，但随后他们会写一些非常私人的事情，他们只是为了那个人自己做的，通常甚至没有其他人知道。让我印象深刻的例子之一

10
00:05:31.000 --> 00:06:02.000
is an older woman whose husband had passed away, and she wrote, I washed his car every week as he used to. Now, that's clearly not an established public ritual that's thousands of years old. Cars don't even exist until fairly recently. So we know that that woman made that ritual up herself. But as you listen to it, you can see how meaningful and important it was to her. And what we find in our research, and this is the good news, is that even those private ones that we make up ourselves, those are associated with less grief and better coping.
是一位老妇人，她的丈夫去世了，她写道，我像他过去一样每周洗一次他的车。现在，这显然不是一个有几千年历史的既定公共仪式。直到最近才甚至有汽车。所以我们知道那个女人自己编造了那个仪式。但当你听它时，你可以看到这对她来说是多么有意义和重要。我们在研究中发现的好消息是，即使是我们自己编造的那些私人的仪式，也与更少的悲伤和更好的应对有关。

